The throwaway consumer culture has become normalized in many product categories despite environmental and economic costs of replacement cycles. Cultural narratives shape purchasing behaviour; shifting messaging from 'buy new' to 'repair and maintain' requires consistent, visible repositioning. Repair messaging directly counters planned obsolescence narratives and builds consumer confidence in product longevity.

When repair is prominently featured in marketing communications, consumer perception of product value changes. Visibility of repair networks and spare parts availability removes friction from repair decisions. Research shows consumers often don't repair products not because they can't, but because they're unaware repair is accessible or economical.